  i'll be talking about firestore key  visualizer which is an exciting new  feature we will be launching the alpha  soon  cloud firestore is built for automatic  scaling and high performance  out of the box still sometimes when we  talk to our customers  we hear a statement such as i'm  experiencing media latency of  hundreds of milliseconds for my lookup  operations is that expected  or i'm experiencing p99 latency of order  of a few seconds for my right operations  is that normal and often the answer is  no  we commonly find these performance  problems to be due to  the incoming traffic violating the  service best practices  let's use an example to explore one of  the ways an implementation might  violate these best practices i'm a new  cloud firestore customer  and i'm working on launching a new api  in this api i would simply  receive documents and store them in my  firestore database  in this case i've implemented a function  that generates the document ids for me  and this function generates these  document ids incrementally  it's worth noting that we also find  these incremental id patterns  often when customers are importing data  from other databases such as mysql  where auto incrementing ids are common  let's see what kind of traffic pattern  this implementation will result too  as you can see in this picture this will  result with sequential write pattern in  my key space  keys are the full paths to your  documents  so for any given document the key is  going to  include all the parent collections in  addition to the document id which is the  last part of the key  firestore is a wrench ordered service at  any point in time  your key space is going to map to a  number of underlying shards  the undesired property of this right  pattern is that at any point in time  your load is focused on a single  underlying shard  cloud firestore supports sequential  loads at  up to 500 requests per second at higher  levels of load this can result in  hottest spot  and for example it can show itself in  your api in the form of elevated  latencies or  errors cloud firestore automatic scaling  capabilities enables you to scale up  your load  to millions millions of requests per  second  more specifically by following our ramp  up guideline which suggests to generally  increase your load by fifty percent  every five minutes you can scale up your  row  your load to tens of millions of  requests per second  and still in a random service a  sequential load pattern continues to map  to a single chart  and hence can result in hot spots  at cloud firestore we are constantly  working to provide you with better  features that can  help you diagnose this type of  performance problems we are excited to  launch key visualizer  in alpha soon this tool provides you  with visibility  into the traffic patterns of your  database across the key  space and helps you self-diagnose a  number of different performance problems  let's go back to the customer story that  we were just reviewing  so in preparation for my api launch i've  set up load tests that can hopefully  represent that  the traffic that i'm going to receive  during after my launch  during this load test i start to observe  that i'm experiencing  some poor performance for my write  operations  i jump to the key visualizer view in  order to start the debugging process  and get an understanding of where the  root cause of the problem might be  cloud firestore key visualizer provides  two dimensional heat maps  of your project's traffic  in this heat map views the horizontal  axis is  time increasing from left to right  and the vertical axis is your key space  with the lower key values located at the  bottom  and your largest keys located at the top  firestore key visualizer heat map views  are two hours wide  the heated scale at the top left  provides the color coding that is used  in this heat map  in particular i can see that black or  dark purple  indicates that the metric value is one  of the lower values  versus the glowing white which indicates  those regions are carrying some of the  higher values for this metric  fire circuit visualizer provides a  number of different metrics  including your total qps  in addition to the qps broken down for  your write  lookup and query operations  it also includes latency metrics for  these three rpc categories  going back to the example that we were  just reviewing i start by examining the  right  latencies metric using the provided  color coding here  i can identify that there is a  sequential load pattern  that is accompanied with elevated levels  of latency  in particular i can find regions  where the average latency can go as high  as a few seconds  this is certainly not good and indicates  that there is a serious performance  degradation happening  next i switch to the right qps metric  using this metric i can observe that  this sequential load pattern  is operating at higher than 5000  requests per second  thinking back about cloud files for best  practices  i recall that sequential load patterns  are well supported up to 500 requests  per second  but otherwise at higher levels of load  they can result in  hot spots and performance problems  i can confirm with my application code  that the document ids within this  collection  are in fact being set sequentially and  this is the likely source and root cause  of this performance problem  in this case i will go ahead and change  my implementation  to use the firestore auto-generated ids  which are randomized by default  and redeploy my code to production  next i can use key visualizer to confirm  that my  traffic is now well distributed across  my key space  and that my latencies have moved back to  the normal range  okay great i was able to use key  visualizer  to efficiently debug and resolve a  performance issue  now there are a number of other use  cases that you can actually use key  visualizer for for performance debugging  for instance in this case as a customer  i'm able to quickly identify that  collection fuel is the one that is  having performance issues  even though my right operations look to  be pretty well distributed in this case  it seems like there is there is another  reason that my rights are performing  poorly  i start by examining the documents which  are being written in this collection  and identify that these documents are  carrying a field  that contains the time of insertion of  the dock  cloud firestore indexes are strongly  consistent  and hence they are updated at the time  of your document rights  this means that if you're doing if  you're performing rights  with documents that have sequential feed  values those can also result in hot  spots  on your indexes in the case that you  have documents with sequential field  values  you should either exempt them from  indexing in the case that you don't need  them for your queries  or in the case that you need them for  your queries you can partition  those heavy write patterns for instance  by applying a random prefix  to those sequential field values in the  case that  you use this technique you would need to  re-aggregate the result  on the read side okay so we reviewed a  couple of examples where you might  experience performance problems  due to violating the best practices  please continue to review  our full list of best practices in order  to make sure you're fully utilizing the  all the high performance capabilities  that are built into cloud firestore and  now i'm going to hand it over to min  to take us home thank you arash  and thank you to all the viewers who  joined us today this concludes our  session  if you haven't signed up for cloud  firestore we hope you take the  opportunity  to get started for free if you have  questions about today's presentation  please feel free to post your questions  on the dory if you'd like to learn more  about how  customers are using cloud firestore i  encourage you to join the new york times  session  which will discuss how they utilize  cloud firestore to power collaborative  editing  and finally we encourage you to join our  large community of developers on stack  overflow  thank you
 

